\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RNPOpenIngMood
\item[* relevant examples:] (I still hesitate about) $_{CL}$[PRO join the 
expedition] $\rightarrow _{NP}$[$_{S}$[PRO joining the expedition]]
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_
Mooddetermination
\item[* date of creation:] 30-AUG-1988 13:44:35  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 08/12/88\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to make OpenIng NPs out of CLAUSEs. It still is unclear in which 
cases the OpenIngMood rule should apply (making an OpenIng SENTENCE), and in 
which cases the current rule (making an OpenIng SENTENCE headed by an NP). 
Especially in constructions with prepositions, 
and in subject position, Jan O.\ seems to favour NPs (his test: if a wh-element 
can be shifted out of the OpenIng, it must be a sentence). 
The Verbpattern Rules have been adapted to that preference.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
